    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "The three-day summit, held August 5 to 7 at the Hyatt Regency Chicago, opened with the Trade Team Invitational, a series of one on one meetings pairing international buyers with US exporters, before moving into a full agenda of keynotes and breakout sessions on global market dynamics, sustainability, and supply chain resilience.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, that demand story has a distinctly local chapter. Sixteen Sri Lankan poultry producers and feed millers currently hold licenses to carry the Sustainable U.S. Soy and Fed with Sustainable U.S. Soy labels, verified under the U.S. Soy Sustainability Assurance Protocol (SSAP), putting Sri Lanka firmly among the countries leading adoption of the label in Asia. New Anthoney\u2019s Farms was the first company in the South Asia region to secure the license, back in 2023, and has since become something of a reference point for USSEC when it talks about what sustainable sourcing looks like in practice.\n\nSri Lankan representatives also joined one of the summit\u2019s sustainability panels, where the message was that sustainability and profitability are not competing goals. Producers spoke of the gains that come from adopting sustainable sourcing practices, and of the Sustainable US Soy logo as a way to showcase that commitment directly to consumers, a visible marker of their dedication to climate action, high-quality nutrition, and protein rich feed sourcing.\n\nThe recognition builds on a year of accelerating institutional ties between Sri Lanka and USSEC. In May, the Sri Lanka Association of Animal Nutrition, known as SLAAN, was formally launched in Kandy in partnership with USSEC, giving the country\u2019s nutritionists and feed scientists their first dedicated professional body.\n\nThe country remains the largest containerised buyer of US soybean meal in the world, importing roughly 255,000 tonnes in 2025, feeding an animal feed industry that now produces close to 1.3 million metric tons annually with poultry as its largest customer."
